#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60f61c26-ff2d-5343-becd-d8f44f623cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Convergence of a Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fe7eb19-ca69-59a9-9b6d-7566e236919b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330516} {:answer/id #uuid "4909ad26-bb57-50e1-98a6-131eb5cf133a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330605} {:answer/id #uuid "49da2403-95d4-5028-8f85-5f62a31c9cf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330612} {:answer/id #uuid "4cafdca7-2613-5ffc-84f2-36ab8973b87d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330648} {:answer/id #uuid "af7daf58-a719-5622-88ca-95e3ffe88e69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49da2403-95d4-5028-8f85-5f62a31c9cf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330612} :answer-field/id #uuid "be89ac6e-eedb-5003-b5ac-a70bcf5e02f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2ee1b3ae-f8d2-59d0-abf6-79ccfd75cdf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-50873" :question/problem "The power series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{(x+2)}^{n}$ converges at  $x=-1.$ Which of the following **must** be true?\n\n- The series converges at $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$\n- The series diverges at $x=-3$\n- The series converges at $x=-\\frac{7}{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The center of the expansion is $x=-2,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-1,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $1,$ i.e., $R\\ge 1.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $(-3,-1].$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $(-3,-1].$\n\n![](images/93/93ab98aaa7170e5fcef1c090555f259b2030384b6f2f25a282af9d8b6a406ed2.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $-\\frac{3}{2}\\in (-3,-1],$ the series is convergent at $x=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $-3\\notin (-3,-1],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is divergent at $x=-3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $-\\frac{7}{2}\\notin (-3,-1],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332835 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60f61c26-ff2d-5343-becd-d8f44f623cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Convergence of a Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32b97c3f-adca-51ec-b80f-f5c554394196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330399} {:answer/id #uuid "444f34fc-7e48-5ef9-b999-ff74c517a5a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330562} {:answer/id #uuid "4c1ba97f-2ad2-5cef-92bf-e83d5e39827b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330641} {:answer/id #uuid "8f947e65-d6fd-5d6a-b7a8-932a6799cf1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331222} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0ab6b7-b358-57b7-a05a-02ac2d49c868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a0ab6b7-b358-57b7-a05a-02ac2d49c868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331331} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cb270b4-c5a8-5c0d-a05d-26f0c75ddc5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "29a54ff7-5783-54c7-b610-5c600dd04d48" :question/math-academy-id "q-50874" :question/problem "Given that the power series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x+1)^{n}$ converges at  $x=-4,$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\n- The series diverges at $x=-5$\n- The series converges at $x=2$\n- The series converges at $x=-3$" :question/worked-solution "The center of the expansion is $x=-1,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-4,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $3,$ i.e., $R\\ge 3.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[-4,2).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[-4,2).$ \n\n![](images/a2/a246a6460d15037b469072b4275e51ff4dd9c56242f6ffeae1bebc2adf66f39f.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $-5\\notin [-4,2),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is divergent at $x=-5.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $2\\notin [-4,2),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $-3\\in [-4,2),$ the series is convergent at $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60f61c26-ff2d-5343-becd-d8f44f623cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Convergence of a Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01724bf3-1dc2-5737-a8da-3d996c4a6d6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329969} {:answer/id #uuid "10c71716-883b-5bad-9700-034e3664f86b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330100} {:answer/id #uuid "76db766b-410d-50dc-9157-b18b41298d88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331012} {:answer/id #uuid "82c8caf8-a47f-5011-b8dd-071a1ea1702d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331108} {:answer/id #uuid "abd33777-e618-539c-86a3-155564eeaba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82c8caf8-a47f-5011-b8dd-071a1ea1702d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331108} :answer-field/id #uuid "a60bc212-bcc7-50fd-9353-0e677715adba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "32c4dcc9-1fba-5e61-8a0a-ad46399f9d53" :question/math-academy-id "q-50876" :question/problem "The power series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x+1)^{n}$ converges at  $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Which of the following **must** be true?\n\n- The series converges at $x=-3$\n- The series converges at $x=-\\frac{7}{2}$\n- The series converges at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "The center of the expansion is $x=-1,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $\\frac{5}{2},$ i.e., $R\\ge \\frac{5}{2}.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $(-\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{3}{2}].$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $(-\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{3}{2}].$\n\n![](images/57/57208948dece1112674d3578763bc8d6b73d1d6ab91d160b14839e16a39cf2f6.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $-3\\in (-\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{3}{2}],$ the series is convergent at $x=-3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $-\\frac{7}{2}\\notin (-\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{3}{2}],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $2\\notin (-\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{3}{2}],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60f61c26-ff2d-5343-becd-d8f44f623cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Convergence of a Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "605f79ee-cf99-57fe-908f-555d59cc7098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330805} {:answer/id #uuid "7354c337-ff34-56f0-a574-86e2810f2670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330989} {:answer/id #uuid "87023c75-e0ac-5288-bcd7-d1fb8cc09705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331154} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb5fb6c-bb7f-52b1-aabf-5fc66c5ba412" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331650} {:answer/id #uuid "e8520aea-c57e-5687-80f7-e3ff8627cecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8520aea-c57e-5687-80f7-e3ff8627cecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331988} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5c48f98-e934-5dbf-9b55-ed0b590ec5f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0bcb862b-db75-545a-8402-c194bc3da35a" :question/math-academy-id "q-50877" :question/problem "The power series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x-3)^{n}$ converges at  $x=-2.$ Which of the following **must** be true?\n\n- The series converges at $x=-3$\n- The series diverges at $x=1$\n- The series converges at $x=8$" :question/worked-solution "The center of the expansion is $x=3,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-2,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $5,$ i.e., $R\\ge 5.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[-2,8).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[-2,8).$\n\n![](images/38/38763aa0d87065a01ccdf0d94bca2a4e1d6ef227aeab4aaacb9703246eee6abf.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $-3\\notin [-2,8),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $1\\in [-2,8),$ the series is convergent at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $8\\notin [-2,8),$  we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the given statements must be true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6e5e228-fa65-531d-a8da-e444079bd9d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent Series Using Multiple Convergence Tests"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "284460a7-ef2f-5e83-ad64-00afa46e4e6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330309} {:answer/id #uuid "3076ab0c-1b37-5f71-b286-4699046008e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330375} {:answer/id #uuid "b809f2b1-70a7-515d-988b-6b26b874fb8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331573} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8f61b9-f0da-5836-a896-faa2323ac688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331625} {:answer/id #uuid "df672869-7677-5995-adde-4a3af68231d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df672869-7677-5995-adde-4a3af68231d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331921} :answer-field/id #uuid "e167b2e2-f96b-546e-8b81-80e30eadd001" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1a500c68-ffb3-5162-a19f-22fd0f314af1" :question/math-academy-id "q-50971" :question/problem "Which of the following series converge?\n\nI. ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}$\n\nII. ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}$\n\nIII. ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "We use the flowchart below to determine which tests to use for each series.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa7f0243efeab51d00aa9694dd018245f7ca4664cc1a5fdf4e646aebb61536e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's test each series in turn.\n\n- For the first series, we use the alternating series test. Observe that \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n!}\n$$\n\nis positive and decreasing for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, by the alternating series test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}\n$$\n\nis also convergent.\n\n- For the second series, we use the limit comparison test. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nWorking out the necessary limit, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}÷\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\cdot \\sqrt{n}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2n}{n+2}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2}{(1+\\frac{2}{n})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $L=2$ is positive and finite. Now, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nis divergent by the $p$-series test. So, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\n$$\n\nis also divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\n- For the final series, we use the $n$th term test for divergence. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we have ${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty ,$ since the polynomial in the numerator grows faster than the polynomial in the denominator. Therefore, by the $n$th term test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nis divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only series I converges."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115380 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "794398c0-ffbf-5971-a411-f236607f56b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Differentiable at a Point Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "661c04c6-f39d-5303-a1c1-315b9d092cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330860} {:answer/id #uuid "6b4be6e4-5a8f-5198-8574-f84193e9fd2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330914} {:answer/id #uuid "719c11e3-2be2-5b5f-9128-f9d7b23390c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330970} {:answer/id #uuid "7effab35-5d94-58d2-ab7f-3fc3bc37bae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331070} {:answer/id #uuid "9e318c2a-f7c8-5253-946c-719d5b57bba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e318c2a-f7c8-5253-946c-719d5b57bba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331365} :answer-field/id #uuid "b952500e-a546-5e39-a3b6-570fb88f1db1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc8d31c1-2a52-5465-9bb5-91b8bdc2abf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-51070" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5eae80c03e35f79eaa484a5d54f7fa1a0ec46a5f27480fb69089efab938eba06.png)\n\nGiven the plot of the function $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The function is continuous at $x=1$\n- The function is differentiable at $x=0$\n- The function is differentiable at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is true. The graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=1$ without taking the pencil off the paper.\n\n- Statement II is false. The function $f(x)$ has a cusp at this point and so it is not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is false. From the graph we can see that $f(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-1$ and so it is not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Simple Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "176784a9-2217-5b8c-8346-fefc27233a66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333198} {:answer/id #uuid "6141caba-dd6e-5f8e-ab20-582224066df4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333876} {:answer/id #uuid "903779b4-ce9e-569a-8a27-0843fdb10ba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334274} {:answer/id #uuid "996a1103-8011-536d-abff-5a58ec376bf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334345} {:answer/id #uuid "b5676e49-a9a8-56dc-8a4d-0c33d7125cbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5676e49-a9a8-56dc-8a4d-0c33d7125cbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334578} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb72c4ef-6aba-5873-a474-26a71328c745" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "347ed2fe-a388-536f-85e7-6e55844b3b57" :question/math-academy-id "q-64252" :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+81y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=81\\,⟹\\,\\omega =9.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a1bfffa-7e6f-5861-bd14-c92f151e7f56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333383} {:answer/id #uuid "5f17494f-3539-5418-92d6-7031577d0ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333855} {:answer/id #uuid "6a66529e-07a6-5f98-831d-30e7058ff421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333962} {:answer/id #uuid "6dad3a85-8fcc-51e8-9b62-ec2e287d342b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333986} {:answer/id #uuid "c94bd2df-30c8-5a23-9e9a-15c700d0ebd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f17494f-3539-5418-92d6-7031577d0ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333855} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3c83a75-aff4-538a-8d26-bd2b543113cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7bb9654d-ae6a-57d3-8c11-b8ef9be232d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-65009" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- ${e}^{x}{y}^{″}+y={x}^{2}$\n- $y{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}=x$\n- $3{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+4{y}^{2}=x$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}{y}^{″}+y={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)={e}^{x},b(x)=0,c(x)=1,$ and $f(x)={x}^{2}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\ny{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $y{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+4{y}^{2}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $4{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29dd6e52-8cc7-5a40-9628-f1492252265f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333379} {:answer/id #uuid "6e496fcc-1702-5c4e-b734-99859afa39c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333991} {:answer/id #uuid "7131df8c-f61e-52dd-859d-6f4570828401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334018} {:answer/id #uuid "8d39ca0a-09d5-508c-aab0-f4c6ec0f7902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334249} {:answer/id #uuid "c78fd17b-56bd-5fd6-be8d-2b44ea796138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e496fcc-1702-5c4e-b734-99859afa39c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333991} :answer-field/id #uuid "6724515a-8eab-580b-bfcf-1ad5d2b783e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7815e919-91eb-5d8d-8ac9-3683413b2268" :question/math-academy-id "q-65110" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}+4y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}$\n- $y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$\n- $y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}$ is **not** a solution of the given equation. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotient of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=1,{c}_{2}=5.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is **not** a solution of the given equation because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335856 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110571 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67692569-e3a4-51e8-99ac-79d8b3081363" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "211c4fdb-6ebe-539d-b252-f52fc26d121c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333287} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8833dc-8aa3-55e7-b0a1-071d4d210778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333965} {:answer/id #uuid "6b39f7de-3469-5659-9d17-3f76b3571a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333969} {:answer/id #uuid "82f0e020-8398-5e80-845f-f70c3cd2c0c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334157} {:answer/id #uuid "cdbb89ca-5b05-580c-a13f-d3b45bdb9efa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b39f7de-3469-5659-9d17-3f76b3571a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333969} :answer-field/id #uuid "9894efbc-2ee1-532c-a190-b2256f27a3a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a75bb764-05ea-55ac-922e-cca49efb6283" :question/math-academy-id "q-65821" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$\n\n- $(0,-2\\pi )$\n- $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\n- $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(0,-2\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-2\\pi ,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=-2\\pi $ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,-2\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110571 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67692569-e3a4-51e8-99ac-79d8b3081363" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21106a7e-9663-5c2f-9493-06ccc4812eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333283} {:answer/id #uuid "54db2333-64f6-5d4b-a7bb-208908845679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333763} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdb4a89-a7f4-5ba5-8196-e668bda1dfd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334097} {:answer/id #uuid "aa80706d-7469-5946-ab7a-634e42040e8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334483} {:answer/id #uuid "d4545ce5-7fec-51c5-850f-0952353bdbce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21106a7e-9663-5c2f-9493-06ccc4812eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333283} :answer-field/id #uuid "7537239a-ce19-5c55-b50d-c27a4c60acce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "53be597d-dd1d-573c-aa44-0038d6375f1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-65822" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$\n\n- $(0,-\\pi )$\n- $(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$\n- $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.  \n\n- First, we consider the point $(0,-\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\pi ,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=-\\pi $ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,-\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{6},-\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335868 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110588 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Graph of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d0182ff-ac06-59ee-b6c7-a1142bf0006d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333102} {:answer/id #uuid "198bfc0e-18bf-5d89-8f54-de876f049e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333222} {:answer/id #uuid "e8fd4dc7-2d19-5d53-98ee-f3e6baaf585b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335023} {:answer/id #uuid "ec8e8af5-2ea2-594d-bf2d-b788ae22ba37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335061} {:answer/id #uuid "f6797427-f4e3-5103-bd17-fcaaf60c5e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "198bfc0e-18bf-5d89-8f54-de876f049e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333222} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a297fbc-d515-5876-8ad7-adb3cf909809" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "abdfc725-c190-5fa3-9b7b-d91b4625d300" :question/math-academy-id "q-65979" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\n- $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\n- $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\n- $(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110588 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Graph of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68775c04-fdbf-55b6-940b-327dbdd92257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333948} {:answer/id #uuid "6a61b6ba-adff-5daf-a0b2-f9fadef5753c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :a