hich the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval  $[-\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$  is closed, but the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,\\pi ]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[0,\\pi ].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$  is not closed, so, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c85ec74-7261-5a84-b268-2cb5156ec3e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339237} {:answer/id #uuid "324afc9c-5fc7-5f92-8306-6b5f8eebe2c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339570} {:answer/id #uuid "696a6442-ff9a-551c-9da2-c842f797cf3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340065} {:answer/id #uuid "85e6b77c-a45a-52cf-b8c9-1652a4b8a948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340328} {:answer/id #uuid "facb3de9-0178-54cd-b07c-1c80bbb75fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "324afc9c-5fc7-5f92-8306-6b5f8eebe2c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339570} :answer-field/id #uuid "217a3a4a-e905-59b0-918b-c92be80eb6b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "122f233b-5382-5fef-8fbd-2b8682266e28" :question/math-academy-id "q-73481" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{x}}{{x}^{2}+1}?$   \n\nI. $[-1,5]$\n\nII. $[0,2]$\n\nIII. $[2,5]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,5]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because $\\sqrt[4]{x}$ is not defined anywhere in the subinterval $[-1,0).$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The intervals $[0,2]$ and $[2,5]$ are closed, and the function is continuous on each of these intervals Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on each $[0,2]$  and $[2,5].$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24811889-2c2a-5489-87b6-7ec9fcc9ec5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339445} {:answer/id #uuid "49559a38-7682-51c6-961d-af8f6d4b9c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339786} {:answer/id #uuid "8e550649-e7ec-521f-b83e-ccfcdfe9298e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340407} {:answer/id #uuid "b182cd8c-78d4-5443-bfc8-d64291dce17e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340743} {:answer/id #uuid "b2f9dc50-f6a9-5e82-a931-a20f17095169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b182cd8c-78d4-5443-bfc8-d64291dce17e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340743} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cc71ef4-f030-5214-97b4-d07a1eb70ea5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe7192f0-0469-5c77-92a1-060194834d30" :question/math-academy-id "q-73482" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{x+1}}?$   \n\n- $[-1,0]$\n- $[1,10]$\n- $(-1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,0]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because it is not defined at $x=-1.$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,10]$ is  closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,10].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval  $(-1,0)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a68717c-3c5f-58a5-9174-e8f45f3e1ef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339216} {:answer/id #uuid "336f07da-9df2-582d-80ca-e44d8eb292b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339585} {:answer/id #uuid "43af0409-9e51-5d17-a8fb-98b37bd00bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339722} {:answer/id #uuid "72581646-406e-5e01-9bba-7ad77de94142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340144} {:answer/id #uuid "8e33f721-3fb7-5e44-8644-c40ab2c07185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "336f07da-9df2-582d-80ca-e44d8eb292b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339585} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a35e850-44d5-5740-b0b8-70909d0b180d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5aba1141-c0a4-54c1-bd64-30eb78786676" :question/math-academy-id "q-73484" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}?$    \n\nI. $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$\n\nII. $[\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n\nIII. $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$  is closed, but the function is not defined on a part of the interval because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$    is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ is  closed, and the function is continuous on $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51327576-3dc9-528f-9e13-d755abf55471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339872} {:answer/id #uuid "7b3f93d2-0ebe-5934-8c91-fce10e0d1450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340232} {:answer/id #uuid "90e67802-13a1-5947-86e3-d0c565f61870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340428} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6c8074-427b-5dfe-9ad1-218b184fe226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340690} {:answer/id #uuid "ef2a0a0c-7790-530c-89f1-dbee9d7d9e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90e67802-13a1-5947-86e3-d0c565f61870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340428} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b5cc245-bbea-5aaf-8a56-ee9acee3fb23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e05c6d3d-29e1-53ab-9c70-1ad2b27199a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-73486" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{x})?$   \n\n- $[\\frac{1}{2},1]$\n- $[0,\\frac{1}{4})$\n- $[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[\\frac{1}{2},1]$   is closed, and the function is continuous on $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$   Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,\\frac{1}{4})$  is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,1]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because it is not defined at $x=0.$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341988 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a506d32-c549-5247-82e3-f237fa06872d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339215} {:answer/id #uuid "22362652-f0bc-5e3c-afa7-25ce82d78661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339421} {:answer/id #uuid "327c0e31-b1ed-5548-81bc-18ff4bca8bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339573} {:answer/id #uuid "87824f92-9edf-52ca-9f8c-b3eeb64b58cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340341} {:answer/id #uuid "a19759de-fa06-5d86-925a-320a7299775b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a19759de-fa06-5d86-925a-320a7299775b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340594} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a8dae27-98ed-5b9c-b45d-d7007b99d819" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96d14ec6-ae06-5360-8ddd-d401dbb48508" :question/math-academy-id "q-73487" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{x}{{2}^{x}-1}?$    \n\nI. $[0,1]$\n\nII. $[0,1)$\n\nIII. $[-2,-1]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[0,1]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because it is not defined at $x=0$ (it makes zero the denominator).   Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval  $[0,1)$   is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-2,-1]$    is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-2,-1].$   Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27cfa7a4-d6c1-5351-99b2-6635044d85f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339474} {:answer/id #uuid "65459a76-323b-5a88-8a2f-2e108e43ea11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340024} {:answer/id #uuid "88971863-2310-5e3f-ac5d-6e4e1adfb7df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340348} {:answer/id #uuid "91912873-03a1-524d-9ad0-7c617923af84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340433} {:answer/id #uuid "be62f3bd-2eeb-5b57-889c-ca65dbe099b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91912873-03a1-524d-9ad0-7c617923af84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340433} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcb8b636-417c-59ba-b9cc-599917ded547" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7b9b884e-eb8a-59c7-aa31-49f0025fb47f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73495" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ -1, & x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-1,1]$\n\nII. $[\\frac{1}{2},1]$\n\nIII. $[-1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[-1,1].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}({2}^{-x})={2}^{0}=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$ In this interval, $f(x)={2}^{-x}$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $[-1,0)$, is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ceb0ce8-da1c-5b99-9182-d772be7cec72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339520} {:answer/id #uuid "7896c996-e406-5136-a44c-25645e86bbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340203} {:answer/id #uuid "9a19840a-be8c-5757-bad3-4daa8391bbb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340517} {:answer/id #uuid "a718fb5b-1dd8-5664-a8f8-13023ada0bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340646} {:answer/id #uuid "f499afb4-f0c7-5505-912f-71dee8dd2498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a19840a-be8c-5757-bad3-4daa8391bbb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340517} :answer-field/id #uuid "141d901b-70e2-5398-8913-6c50c5c4a073" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d1eaad6e-884d-57bd-a4ae-a690d3d6abe9" :question/math-academy-id "q-73499" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1), & x>1 \\\\ x-1, & x\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[0,2]$\n\nII. $[-1,0]$\n\nIII. $[-1,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[0,2].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[-1,0].$ In this interval, $f(x)=x-1$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,0].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, in the interval $[-1,2]$, we also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,2]$ and therefore  the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "235e2186-c516-5a12-99e1-855472aad9f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339433} {:answer/id #uuid "7e1ad79a-9054-5adb-8e27-82c74fb0eeee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340265} {:answer/id #uuid "d65d9629-6375-5d3b-9bf7-be4b8ce2e02b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341027} {:answer/id #uuid "de97e50a-3ac0-5909-8788-42a53cdaa913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341105} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a1b048-a2c7-5d2d-99eb-e4c76eea7395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e1ad79a-9054-5adb-8e27-82c74fb0eeee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340265} :answer-field/id #uuid "3883c8f8-56cb-5ac2-9602-b5a9a360bba9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a761269-f6ea-50eb-8e45-58983c3d9692" :question/math-academy-id "q-73503" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ \\,\\,2, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-1,1]$\n- $(0,3]$\n- $[-2,-1]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- First, let's consider $[-1,1].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}=\\frac{2-0}{{2}^{0}+1}=1\\ne f(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[-2,-1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[-2,-1].$  Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[-2,-1],$  and the extreme value theorem is also applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f8ca629-0927-538d-ab6c-ffef86ed4c3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339401} {:answer/id #uuid "3cbeae54-1a8e-537b-bcc8-b644943612ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339669} {:answer/id #uuid "4680f954-f4f8-5258-ae55-a7412a19432c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339754} {:answer/id #uuid "bfda1598-8520-5bca-930a-7ba3397c4c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340839} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb6ae8f-aab7-5bdc-8f7c-dc14885c5a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cbeae54-1a8e-537b-bcc8-b644943612ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339669} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c36c391-b98e-5750-8019-3f694dde0b5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dcdd6cca-dc15-5e0c-a55e-4d73a59866ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-73504" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2{x}^{3}-1, & x<1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡(x-1), & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-1,2]$\n- $(1,3]$\n- $[2,\\pi ]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[-1,2].$  We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(2{x}^{3}-1)=2(1)^{3}-1=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}{sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)={sin}^{2}⁡(1-1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(1,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[2,\\pi ].$ In this interval, $f(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,\\pi ].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085a372e-3d44-5218-8ffc-7245e73f20e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339197} {:answer/id #uuid "27bfa14d-cc0f-5a6b-9127-444c2c3cbe94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339472} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff1f004-8a24-53f6-8512-8daed863b9af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339982} {:answer/id #uuid "807a16d0-c898-5c5a-aa54-af34b1d71126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340280} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef8375b-1f86-5526-a7e2-474095047082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "807a16d0-c898-5c5a-aa54-af34b1d71126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340280} :answer-field/id #uuid "1167d122-71e9-5a13-8edb-8b2e825eb1e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8abc4b9f-caf8-5c94-91da-62ad0ae61b3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-73531" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{x-1}-1}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ \\,\\,1, & x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-2,2]$\n\nII. $[-3,1)$\n\nIII. $[1.5,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-2,2].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{2}^{x-1}-1}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-3,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\n- Finally consider  $[1.5,2].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=1,$ which is not in the interval  $[1.5,2].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[1.5,2]$, and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f079a8-3eef-5e0c-b508-56b0a28e810a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339187} {:answer/id #uuid "33e2246e-0cb8-5b3d-a663-6ded80537d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339590} {:answer/id #uuid "36e9f6d3-ade4-54f2-83b8-98b440f7e2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339621} {:answer/id #uuid "9be8a799-d92f-523e-80f4-9f217caeda37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340530} {:answer/id #uuid "a94a7551-868d-5a9d-8d58-61cf61d367d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9be8a799-d92f-523e-80f4-9f217caeda37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340530} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c233766-89ba-51e9-91fa-b4ab683f8678" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "451e02c3-10e2-5101-a83f-538a8be6b03d" :question/math-academy-id "q-73549" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the ellipses $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1?$\n\n- It is a type I region\n- It is a type II region\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/62/627e7e102a0f16cb0c4946e42b48ad122299d729c5bc419293e258a3f2e1701e.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.25$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](images/d7/d7aa48c887d6fb1c3d0420867b7e74010fa93e16d7e24317e2cf214764e56113.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=0.5$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/ec/ec632fa5f86d8d9b34b9ccf661a3219e965f289c28bf69d7d80eeeb46093e7ae.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db4180e-9e56-5615-a261-a06b057afbc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339254} {:answer/id #uuid "44382309-a210-5068-b421-83edd371f540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339728} {:answer/id #uuid "705bf110-f45f-5748-8977-a6e4eaab55d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340124} {:answer/id #uuid "a661de8b-e986-5edb-ae2a-54d4fbff884d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340638} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1d59d6-b1c0-5ab1-b672-2772922e372b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "705bf110-f45f-5748-8977-a6e4eaab55d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340124} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4646991-5d1f-5339-9e5a-2aa1bae21d22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e93e600-4a01-5ada-842d-9b175b57ee56" :question/math-academy-id "q-74217" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:1-x-y=0\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:2x+3y-z=0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $1-x-y=0$ since $1-0-0\\ne 0.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace. Indeed, the vector $u=⟨1,0,0⟩$ is in $B$, but $2u=⟨2,0,0⟩$ is not in $B$, since ${2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}≰1.$ So, $B$ is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $2x+3y-z=0$ since $2\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43ab7da0-2f07-50d1-831b-c157d66b1de8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339721} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffb62c9-06bc-54ed-a237-28e9635fc6fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340121} {:answer/id #uuid "72703321-1679-5b08-aab6-32e985e25b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340146} {:answer/id #uuid "74517a98-ee70-5879-af22-ee6f1cbffcda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340165} {:answer/id #uuid "ef265337-e4c5-5117-bd72-923b375019cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74517a98-ee70-5879-af22-ee6f1cbffcda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340165} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa75a06c-11fb-5ef6-a1f6-3f25d8c6ebf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5037faf6-6f8a-5ea2-800f-2c136a3bb59e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74218" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1}$\n- $B={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:xyz=0}$\n- $C={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:y=-1}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ since ${0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it is not closed under vector addition. Indeed, both $a=⟨1,0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,1,1⟩$ belong to $B$ but \n\n$$\na+b=⟨1,0,1⟩+⟨0,1,1⟩=⟨1,1,2⟩\n$$\n\n \ndoes not satisfy the condition $xyz=0$ since $1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\ne 0.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $y=-1$ since $0\\ne -1.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3171ea47-31ac-55ef-8e65-7c8cc13d17db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339563} {:answer/id #uuid "4780f3c7-7445-5e20-b679-8d09700f5b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339761} {:answer/id #uuid "69308677-f0aa-523b-9be3-7371c6dc3d65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340063} {:answer/id #uuid "7458caf1-0896-52f4-b93d-16c6705c4441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340166} {:answer/id #uuid "962e504c-ea04-57e3-acb7-34e1af23490d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69308677-f0aa-523b-9be3-7371c6dc3d65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340063} :answer-field/id #uuid "df4036f4-0b20-53ba-89f9-e6eee6e466b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb81e4a8-c5a7-59da-b168-b278b747b24a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74255" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}<\\frac{1}{2}\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:5x-y-z=0\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:5-x-z=0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace. Indeed, the vector $u=⟨\\frac{1}{4},0,0⟩$ is in $A$, since\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}<\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nbut, $4u=⟨1,0,0⟩$ is not in $B$, since \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}>\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $5x-y-z=0$ since $5\\cdot 0-0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $5-x-z=0$ since $5-0-0\\ne 0.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74d47c5c-d9b2-5729-b276-f386649b4836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343248} {:answer/id #uuid "78689969-fa12-5d79-b86b-75a95aac6c30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343278} {:answer/id #uuid "93f165e9-4fa8-5327-af87-0aecc43a6729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343532} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d03412-f273-52d2-a7f8-85526134a1c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343815} {:answer/id #uuid "f7884f74-457b-566e-ac8e-ae3ebcb6b395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93f165e9-4fa8-5327-af87-0aecc43a6729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343532} :answer-field/id #uuid "196a3494-8261-5fa3-924a-5e54ae02aac6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5fe00a2-ab56-5281-b3c2-6fe8dad2417a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74964" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}\n$$\n\nI. The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$\n\nII. The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$\n\nIII. The limit exists and is equal to $2$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}-0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x(2x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{2x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot 0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is also true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b9ca939-c7a6-5aeb-98c2-585d48ff8c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342604} {:answer/id #uuid "5716cd24-5a2c-5544-92f6-203e6a676437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342963} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2c0063-1ae4-55ca-87d1-b65a1e62305f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343173} {:answer/id #uuid "731035c9-6220-52ed-886c-05ae731b34ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343235} {:answer/id #uuid "cf56f8d6-4634-5189-80bd-37357cd208d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b9ca939-c7a6-5aeb-98c2-585d48ff8c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342604} :answer-field/id #uuid "51ec1b91-00af-5481-ab92-af724f5e618b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89a636cd-a216-5d73-bd3f-7f9cbe4badf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-75116" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $C?$\n\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $C,$ we write $C=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nC=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form  because  the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains a number not equal to $1.$\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ada63ec-fa1d-5ff7-b669-92737dcdbd74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342330} {:answer/id #uuid "1752c8df-b894-5390-b173-aa620c63a4ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342443} {:answer/id #uuid "409dfa57-961d-5f83-aa15-10d15babbe22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342771} {:answer/id #uuid "467b7351-7ecb-539b-ba6e-6ea1995a8925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342818} {:answer/id #uuid "98410b31-6e91-581a-a43b-5311f6c98db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "467b7351-7ecb-539b-ba6e-6ea1995a8925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342818} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c138955-72ce-5ce7-a05a-28705f2a9e68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0305a43d-bf81-5ecb-8a77-49a30ade0147" :question/math-academy-id "q-75117" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:  \n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as it has an entry not equal to $1$  on the main diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal. \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "454ea782-196e-50f9-b1e2-00d2d1bb0539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342808} {:answer/id #uuid "4f08aad3-17c0-5aea-a1e3-8bc3cab0aea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342888} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2b2691-bd26-5b75-bf4a-0ab5f6a47c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342891} {:answer/id #uuid "567c9971-93d6-5157-ae62-fc0890790260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342958} {:answer/id #uuid "9b54534f-b827-5c6c-a649-e71657a1ca05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "567c9971-93d6-5157-ae62-fc0890790260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342958} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc036018-08d5-5220-9a04-6989a4c32339" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34143660-ecfa-5585-b65e-76b215171706" :question/math-academy-id "q-75123" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $C?$\n\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $C,$ we write $C=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nC=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry above the main diagonal. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as it has an entry not equal to $1$ on the main diagonal.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f2376a1-da00-5911-b146-c5236752448e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342890} {:answer/id #uuid "58038b76-6478-52e3-88f8-675c4aa414d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342974} {:answer/id #uuid "59103f60-0dff-5bf6-af9c-6267f890a47e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342987} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea948d9-d1d3-5312-9f2b-e7b8089e8034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343638} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7d0947-9c9d-5773-b575-44075078af0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ea948d9-d1d3-5312-9f2b-e7b8089e8034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343638} :answer-field/id #uuid "f466101d-ee92-54d3-b4e2-a9cca8e95913" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21485ec9-747a-5129-ba2c-794c570827bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-75496" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :